//! The C interface the JUCE adapter is driven by.
//!
//! A plugin crate exports its [`Plugin`] with
//! [`export_cabi!`](crate::export_cabi) and builds as a static library. Its
//! one symbol, `seam_plugin`, answers a [`SeamPlugin`]: a table of the
//! plugin's facts and of every call into an instance, as `seam.h` declares
//! it. One generic JUCE adapter (`adapters/juce`) links that library and
//! turns it into the formats JUCE builds.
//!
//! - The header is generated by [`header`](fn@header) from the same
//!   declarations the Rust types come from, and `cargo xtask gen` commits it as
//!   `crates/host/include/seam.h`. Header and library are always built from one
//!   commit, so the interface promises no compatibility beyond [`ABI_VERSION`].
//! - Every call in the table is an `unsafe extern "C"` function. Its
//!   contract is its [`SeamPlugin`] field's doc, with the pointer rules at
//!   the top of `seam.h`.
//! - [`juce_settings`] generates the adapter's build settings per plugin.
//! - Which thread may make which call, and when the host's callbacks run, is
//!   [`SeamPlugin`]'s to say. A call it refuses, like a panic, is counted as a
//!   fault ([`boundary_faults`](crate::boundary_faults)).
//!
//! With the `test-host` feature, `TestHost` drives a plugin through the
//! table in a test.

mod abi;
mod header;
mod instance;
mod juce;
#[cfg(any(test, feature = "test-host"))]
mod test_host;

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests;

